{0 Caravan}

Durable, distributed background jobs for OCaml 5 and Eio.

Producers enqueue jobs into shared storage; worker {e nodes} on any number of
machines claim them, run them with retries and timeouts, and record the
outcome. Caravan provides scheduling, priorities, unique jobs, cron, queue
pausing, graceful shutdown, crash recovery, a dashboard and Prometheus
metrics.

{1 Guarantees}

- {b At-least-once delivery.} A job whose node crashes mid-attempt is rescued
  and run again elsewhere. A job can therefore run more than once: make
  [perform] idempotent.
- {b No double-claiming.} Two nodes never hold the same attempt of a job.
  Outcomes are recorded against a {!Caravan.Row.claim}, so a node wrongly
  presumed dead cannot overwrite the result of the job's next attempt.
- {b Transactional enqueue} with PostgreSQL: a job exists if and only if the
  transaction that enqueued it commits.

{1 Modules}

{2 Defining and enqueueing jobs}
- {!Caravan.Job}: job definitions
- {!Caravan.Codec}: argument encoding
- {!Caravan.Outcome}: what a job reports
- {!Caravan.Ctx}: the execution context
- {!Caravan.Backoff}: retry delays
- {!Caravan.Client}: enqueue and manage jobs

{2 Running workers}
- {!Caravan.Node}: the worker runtime
- {!Caravan.Middleware}: wrap execution
- {!Caravan.Telemetry}: runtime events
- {!Caravan.Cron}: cron expressions

{2 Storage}
- {!Caravan.Backend}: the storage interface
- {!Caravan.Memory_backend}: in-memory backend for tests and development
- {!Caravan.State}, {!Caravan.Row}: the job lifecycle and record
- The [caravan-postgres] package: the production backend

{1 Lifecycle}

A job starts [Available] (or [Scheduled] if it has a future run time). A node
claims it ([Executing]). The outcome moves it to [Completed], to [Retryable]
(with backoff) or [Discarded] on failure, back to [Scheduled] on snooze, or to
[Cancelled]. The cluster leader stages due jobs back to [Available], rescues
jobs from dead nodes and prunes old finished jobs. See {!Caravan.State} for
the exact transition table.
